{com}. 
. 
. /* Due to some errors in the original coding of the "result" variable, we merge a "newresult" variable with
> the following values:
> missing value if test was not valid due to an email address error
> NRNR if both candidates got no response
> NRNo if cand1 got no response and cand2 got an explicit no
> NRYes if cand1 got no response and cand2 got an explicit yes
> NoNR if cand1 got an explicit no and cand2 got no response
> NoNo if both candidates got an explicit no
> NoYes if cand1 got an explicit no and cand2 got an explicit yes
> YesNR if cand1 got an explicit yes and cand2 got no response
> YesNo if cand1 got an explicit yes and cand2 got an explicit no
> YesYes if both candidates got an explicit yes */
. 
. joinby testnumber using "/Users/claireadida/Desktop/France Documents/March 2009/Analysis/Experiments/CV/NewAdidaCoding.dta", _merge(newresultvariableadded)
{txt}
{com}. 
. /* There are different ways to interpret and code the data. We do the analysis with all possible codings
> First, we follow the ISM-Corum interpretation, which is that cases with 2 explicit NO's and cases with 
> 2 NON-ANSWER's (or a mix of the two) are NOT USABLE. According to them, when there are two explicit NO's, it may mean that the 2 CV's
> were inadequate for the job. When there are 2 non-answers, it may be that the employer has not even considered
> the CV's: in both cases, these are not real tests. The only usable tests are cases with two explicit YES, and
> cases with one explicit YES. Note that this version treats as the same result the following two cases:
> 1 explicit yes and 1 non-response; 1 explicit yes and 1 explicit no. Note that in all cases the control candidate
> is candidate 2 and the test candidate is candidate 1 */
. 
. codebook newresult

{txt}{hline}
{res}newresult{right:(unlabeled)}
{txt}{hline}

